home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-103554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-103554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f03af7a1550f93a859827dcb9678a9d79dd79007f48b332b2f0b1f95fdbe735"} {"question" "q-103554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-103554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{e}^{xy}=x"] ["math" "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{x}^{3}=\\sqrt{1+y}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-103554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f03af7a1550f93a859827dcb9678a9d79dd79007f48b332b2f0b1f95fdbe735"} {"question" "q-331727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=2$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(4x-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f03af7a1550f93a859827dcb9678a9d79dd79007f48b332b2f0b1f95fdbe735"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f03af7a1550f93a859827dcb9678a9d79dd79007f48b332b2f0b1f95fdbe735"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{2}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{3}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f03af7a1550f93a859827dcb9678a9d79dd79007f48b332b2f0b1f95fdbe735"} {"question" "q-331724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-({x}^{2}+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4f03af7a1550f93a859827dcb9678a9d79dd79007f48b332b2f0b1f95fdbe735"} {"question" "q-331739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "q-331739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7b3cf5ecd35dce5dc5b1457a60886b46030397801921bb369c7af1dcd10b9d9"} {"question" "e-6599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/example-6599.json" "file_sha256" "c28f43cb4176204c0753e5c2d81d908983ccf04e94867ff2ca1db4362f7d0800"} {"question" "e-6599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/example-6599.json" "file_sha256" "c28f43cb4176204c0753e5c2d81d908983ccf04e94867ff2ca1db4362f7d0800"} {"question" "e-6600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/example-6600.json" "file_sha256" "5d83eab93feb1087411d62c97fac638e65e1bf091c8470caba835b52f682d535"} {"question" "e-6600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/example-6600.json" "file_sha256" "5d83eab93feb1087411d62c97fac638e65e1bf091c8470caba835b52f682d535"} {"question" "e-21813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/example-21813.json" "file_sha256" "325a147b1975bcc2e08cc644c38a9e5bacd8cdd1660c2b871f64e14b23c11c91"} {"question" "e-21813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071102/example-21813.json" "file_sha256" "325a147b1975bcc2e08cc644c38a9e5bacd8cdd1660c2b871f64e14b23c11c91"}] "usage" {"q-331605" [] "e-21813" [] "q-103554" [] "e-6599" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283026}] "q-16400" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283027} {"kind" "responses" "entity" 17592186283027}] "q-8359" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283029} {"kind" "responses" "entity" 17592186283029}] "q-103551" [] "q-331738" [] "e-6600" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283030}] "q-8349" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283070} {"kind" "responses" "entity" 17592186283070}]} "basis" 2542 "retractions" [[17592186087973 :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3\\cos x = 1 - 4y\\sin x$"] [17592186087973 :question/worked-solution "The standard form is $y^{\\prime}+P(x)y=Q(x)$. Move the $y$ term to the left and the cosine term to the right: $2y^{\\prime}+4y\\sin x=1+3\\cos x$. Dividing every term by $2$ gives $y^{\\prime}+(2\\sin x)y=\\frac{1+3\\cos x}{2}$."] [17592186088362 :question/worked-solution "The equation $y^{\\prime}+2xy=6x^2$ is first-order and linear: $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=6x^2$. The other choices contain $(y^{\\prime})^2$, a second derivative, $y^2$, or the product $yy^{\\prime}$, each of which violates first-order linear form."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186084850 17592186084851 17592186084852 17592186084853 17592186084854 17592186087103 17592186087104 17592186087105 17592186087106 17592186087107 17592186087115 17592186087116 17592186087117 17592186087118 17592186087119 17592186295544 17592186295545 17592186295546 17592186295547 17592186295548 17592186295572 17592186295573 17592186295574 17592186295575 17592186295576 17592186295593 17592186295594 17592186295595 17592186295596 17592186295597 17592186320868 17592186320869 17592186320870 17592186320871 17592186320872] "immutable_fields" [[17592186084849 :answer-field/correct] [17592186084849 :answer-field/id] [17592186084849 :answer-field/key] [17592186084849 :answer-field/type] [17592186087102 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087102 :answer-field/id] [17592186087102 :answer-field/key] [17592186087102 :answer-field/type] [17592186087114 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087114 :answe